// A built-in type constructor takes a fixed number of type arguments — `List` one, `Map` two — and
// applying it to the wrong number is E0058, the same code an over- or under-supplied turbofish
// reports. The arity comes from `BuiltinTy::arity`, so annotations, attribute arguments, and every
// other type-reference position share one rule.
//
// Omitting the arguments entirely stays legal: `x: List` is an inference hole the checker fills
// forward, not an error, and the bare `list`/`map`/`set` spellings *are* the unspecified-element
// form. Only a written argument list of the wrong length is diagnosed — see the accepted cases below,
// which must not warn.
// expect: error E0058 at 15:11
// expect: exit 1
fn ok_bare(a: list, b: map, c: set): void { return }
fn ok_full(a: List<int>, b: Map<string, int>, c: Result<int, string>): void { return }

fn bad(x: Map<int>): void { return }
